"Qualifying Open-Weights Model" means a neural network model whose complete, un-encrypted, un-crippled full-precision training/inference parameter tensors (e.g., `bf16`/`fp16`/`fp32` `.safetensors`), architecture configurations (`config.json`), and tokenizer vocabularies (`tokenizer.json`) are publicly downloadable free of charge by the general public from a public network server without NDA, discretionary manual approval gate, or prohibition (other than Permitted Upstream Open-Weights Community Terms) against commercial use, weight modification, or public redistribution of modified weights and cartridges.

- **"Qualifying Open-Weights Field of Use"** means the field of making, modifying, compiling, executing, and serving covered works **exclusively** on Qualifying Open-Weights Models where all resulting Derived Model Weights and Corresponding Source are publicly released in full compliance with Sections 1, 2, 5, 6, and 13.
- **"Reserved Proprietary & Closed-Weights Field of Use"** means the strictly disjoint, excluded field of all uses involving any closed-weight, proprietary, internal-only, NDA-gated, weights-withheld, or non-redistributable model (including transitive synthetic-data, verifier, or relay pipelines under Section 0(2)). Zero copyright or patent license is granted under This License within the Reserved Proprietary & Closed-Weights Field of Use.

To "modify" a work means to copy from, load into memory for adaptation, translate, or adapt all or part of the Program in a manner requiring Copyright permission under `17 U.S.C. § 106`, or to compile, synthesize, widen, patch, or alter neural network model weights using the Program or (during the patent term) Patent Rights. The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the earlier work or a work "based on" the earlier work.

"Derived Model Weights" means:
1. Any neural network model weights, compiled weight cartridges (`WireCartridgeManifestJson`), neuron slot replacements, widened projection matrices, LoRA/sparse adapters, parameter tensors, or model checkpoints produced, compiled, modified, routed, or executed in whole or in part using the Program or Patent Rights (whether produced domestically or offshore); **and**
2. **Transitive Distillation, Verifier, & Relay-Laundering Closure:** Any downstream neural network model weights trained, pre-trained, mid-trained, fine-tuned, distilled, quantized, merged, calibrated, aligned, or whose training/post-training corpora, trajectories, or rollouts were generated, scored, filtered, ranked, verified, concolically explored, reward-modeled, rejection-sampled, proctored, or curriculum-selected in whole or in part using synthetic data, tool traces, evaluations, filter decisions, activations, hidden states, or logits generated by the Program **or by any Derived Model Weights (transitively across one or more generations of intermediate open-weight or closed-weight checkpoints, verifiers, reward models, or synthetic datasets)**.

A "covered work" means the unmodified Program, a modified version of the Program, or (for purposes of the scope conditions, Patent Rights Field-of-Use limits, and Section 9 contractual covenants herein) any Derived Model Weights.

To "propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without permission, would make you directly, contributorily, or by inducement liable for infringement or breach under applicable Copyright law (`17 U.S.C. §§ 106, 117`), Patent Rights (upon publication/issuance under `35 U.S.C. §§ 154(d), 271(a)–(c), (f), (g)` and `19 U.S.C. § 1337`), or Section 9, including copying into RAM/VRAM for execution, distribution, making available to the public, compiling or hosting Derived Model Weights, and serving or routing inference over a network.

To "convey" a work means any kind of propagation that enables other parties (including customers, end users, B2B API callers, or downstream services) to make or receive copies or to interact (directly or indirectly) with a covered work or Derived Model Weights.

An interactive user interface, checkpoint repository, dataset card, or network API displays "Appropriate Legal Notices" when it prominently includes: (1) an appropriate copyright notice and the virtual patent marking notice **`Patent Pending — Patent Notice: https://github.com/inanna-malick/portia/blob/main/LICENSE`** (`USPTO # 64/194,682`), (2) a notice that there is no warranty (except as expressly provided), that covered works are governed by This License (including a prohibition against closed-weight training, distillation, or relay laundering under Sections 0(2) and 2), and (3) a direct, no-charge public URL to view This License and download complete Corresponding Source (including all base and Derived Model Weights). *(For programmatic HTTP/gRPC/stdio APIs and CLI tools, providing these notices and download URLs via HTTP response headers such as `Link` or `X-Portia-License`, `/v1/models` metadata endpoints, CLI `--version`/`--help` output, and public API documentation fully satisfies this requirement without altering structured payload schemas.)*

### 1. Source Code and Corresponding Source (Anti-Crippling & Quantizer Safe Harbor).

The "source code" for a work means the preferred form of the work for making modifications to it. "Object code" means any non-source form of a work, including compiled binary executables, bytecode, and serialized neural network parameter or cartridge files (`.safetensors`, `.gguf`, `.pt`, JSON weight manifests, or checkpoint shards).

A "Standard Interface" means an official standard defined by a recognized standards body or a widely used programming-language interface. "System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, other than the work as a whole, that (a) is included in packaging a Major Component (OS kernel, window system, hardware driver, or standard language compiler) without being part of that Major Component, and (b) serves only to enable use of the work with that Major Component or implement a publicly available Standard Interface. **A neural network model whose weights are compiled, modified, distilled, evaluated, or executed using the Program is never a System Library; its complete weights, cartridges, and runtime metadata are mandatory parts of the Corresponding Source.**

The "Corresponding Source" for a work in object code or model weight form means all source code, algebraic/grammar specifications, and **complete, un-obfuscated, un-crippled neural network model artifacts** needed to generate, load, run, reproduce, and modify the work and any Derived Model Weights at full numerical and behavioral parity using standard open-source toolchains, including:
1. Complete base model weights and all compiled weight cartridges, widened MLP tensors, slot replacements, or adapters in a standard, documented, un-encrypted format (`.safetensors` at full training/inference precision such as `bf16`/`fp16`/`fp32`, without post-hoc lossy quantization used to withhold the true weights; *provided, however, that where a downstream redistributor merely quantizes an already-public full-precision Qualifying Open-Weights checkpoint into `.gguf`/`FP8`/`AWQ`/`INT4` without additional closed training or distillation, prominently linking to the canonical public full-precision upstream checkpoint repository alongside publishing the quantization recipe satisfies full-precision base weight disclosure*);
2. All model architecture definitions, configuration files (`config.json`), and tokenizer files (`tokenizer.json`, `tokenizer_config.json`);
3. All span-cartridge routing schedules, grammar specifications (`.lark`, JSON Schemas), and compilation manifests (`WireCartridgeManifestJson`, `SpanCartridge` schedules, and bisimulation receipts); and
4. All runtime loader, harness, and compilation scripts required to execute and reproduce the Derived Model Weights without proprietary or closed-source dependencies.

The Corresponding Source for a work in source code form is that same work.

